WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.635 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> (host)
 What looks like
 a reptile amphibian hybrid,</i>

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:06.439 align:start position:0%
<i>has a dish shaped skull,
smells like fish, is childlike,</i>

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:09.174
<i> and out to steal your crops
 and drown your livestock?</i>

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.778
<i>The Japanese water yokai, kappa.</i>

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.379
<i> Kappa translates
 to "river child,"</i>

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:16.215 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> but there are more than
 100 variations of the name,</i>

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:18.217
<i> and while described as
 childlike in appearance,</i>

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.352 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> their behavior
 certainly is not.</i>

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:23.123
Kappa is a truly
Japanese yokai.

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:24.524
While some yokai
originate in China

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.926
or are inspired by
Chinese folklore,

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:29.162
the kappa is Japan
through and through.

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.265
And kappas are obsessed with
butts, so hang on to yours.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:37.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
 and this is "Monstrum."</i>

00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:40.473 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> With so many different
 stories of kappa out there,</i>

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:42.275 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> I knew I would need
 some extra help</i>

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.711 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> digging into the lore
 of this yokai.</i>

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:46.546 line:0%
My name is Yoshiko Okuyama.

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:50.817 line:0%
I'm a full professor
of Japanese studies

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.620 line:0%
at the University
of Hawaii at Hilo.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.625
And I'm also the author of
"Japanese Mythology in Film."

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.329 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Personality-wise, the kappa
 is like the class clown,</i>

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.264 line:0%
<i> with a penchant for pranks.</i>

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:08.300 line:0%
<i> Sometimes, a really bad one.</i>

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.538 line:0%
<i> According to legend, living
 in rivers, ponds, and lakes.</i>

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:15.041 align:start size:91% position:9%
Kappa are commonly
portrayed as green in color

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:16.776 align:start size:78% position:22%
with a turtle shell
on their back,

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:20.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
partially hollowed-out heads,
and beak-like mouths.

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:22.015
<i> A popular kappa
 story is based on</i>

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> a real Japanese feudal lord,
 Kato Kiyomasa.</i>

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.453
<i> After a kappa drowns one
 of his favorite pages,</i>

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:31.023
<i> he orders the execution of all
 the kappas in his province.</i>

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:33.860 align:start position:0%
<i>Buddhist priests chanted to
keep the creatures in the area,</i>

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.295
<i> monkeys were brought in
 to frighten the kappa,</i>

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:38.064 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> and all the streams
 were poisoned</i>

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.766
<i> and filled with hot rocks.</i>

00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:43.369
<i> Scared and sick, the leader
 of the kappa promised his kind</i>

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:46.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> would never harm anyone
 in the region again.</i>

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:48.274
<i> They were forgiven
 and kept their word.</i>

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.610 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is a common trope
in kappa tales.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
Oftentimes, kappa must promise
to stop harassing people,

00:01:54.146 --> 00:01:56.549 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they must offer help
to people when needed

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:59.051 align:start size:81% position:19%
or share their secret
medical remedies.

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:00.853 align:start size:78% position:22%
The kappas were
especially adept at

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:02.588
bone setting, oddly enough.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.191
Their medical knowledge
may connect back to other

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.627 align:start size:88% position:13%
water deities associated
with fertility,

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.295
or agricultural deities,

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.464
especially in more
positive legends,

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:12.565 align:start size:91% position:9%
since they were also given
offerings in the hopes

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:16.069
of securing good
health in children.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.537
<i> Outside of making threats,</i>

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.806 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> there are a few other
 ways to survive an attack.</i>

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.341
<i> Sometimes, a kappa attack
 on a human or livestock</i>

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:24.309
<i> is foiled when the yokai's arm</i>

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.378
<i> is pulled out of their bodies.</i>

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:29.048 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Luckily for kappa,
 it can be reattached.</i>

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:31.284 align:start size:81% position:19%
But they have
a critical weakness.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:34.353
If they spill any of the liquid
from their unique head dish,

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:36.022
it's going to be a problem.

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.691
Let's talk about
that for a second.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:41.527 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The kappa's head has
 a divot that holds a liquid</i>

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> directly related to
 the kappa's life force.</i>

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:48.066
<i> You can defend against a kappa
 attack by wrestling the kappa</i>

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.902
<i>or just by showing some manners.</i>

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:52.638 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> If you bow before your
 opponent, it will bow back,</i>

00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:54.674 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> spilling the contents
 of its head dish</i>

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:57.910 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> and losing its strength,
 and maybe even its own life.</i>

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.214
<i> Another bizarre attribute of
 the kappa, it loves cucumbers,</i>

00:03:02.314 --> 00:03:03.549
<i> which is also the reason why</i>

00:03:03.649 --> 00:03:06.319 line:0%
<i> a cucumber sushi roll
 is called kappamaki,</i>

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:09.322 line:0%
<i> a fun little reference to
 this mischievous yokai.</i>

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:11.624 align:start size:91% position:9%
Less fun fact, if the kappa
pulls you underwater,

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:14.360 align:start size:81% position:19%
it will steal
your liver or blood.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.195
They will often
do so by a rather

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:18.731
non-traditional backdoor method.

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:22.568 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
Why? Well, they must remove
the shirikodama first.

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:24.871 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
<i> The shirikodama is
 a mythical ball-like organ</i>

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:28.575 line:0%
<i> or jewel that people have
 inside their derrieres.</i>

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:31.076 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
<i> (Yoshiko)
 That's the source
 of your vitality,</i>

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:33.312 line:0%
<i> like your life's battery pack.</i>

00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:35.548 line:0%
<i> And Mr. Kappa wants it.</i>

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:39.418 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> Once it snatches your
 shirikodama, you are out.</i>

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:40.586 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
<i> Your body
 becomes like a noodle,</i>

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:44.357 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
<i> all weak and floppy
 in the water.</i>

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:47.994 line:0%
<i> Ah, talk about a bizarre way
 to lose your mojo, right?</i>

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:50.830 align:start size:91% position:9%
And a way to make it easier
for kappa to drown you.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
Apparently, this is
a tasty snack for kappa,

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:56.435 align:start size:97% position:3%
who will consume it or take it
to their king as tribute.

00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:58.771
And yes, some read
earlier kappa lore

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:02.174 align:start size:88% position:13%
as symbolic of male-male
sexual relationships.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:04.610
There are a lot of kappa
stories involving butts,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.646
but let's save that
for the comments.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:09.481 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The kappa is often associated
 not just with violence</i>

00:04:09.581 --> 00:04:12.451
<i> and thievery, but sexual
 harassment and assault.</i>

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:14.553
<i> Kappa are almost always male,</i>

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:15.621
<i>especially in the earlier lore.</i>

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.591 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> According to author
 Kunio Yanagita's</i>

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.826
<i> folklore collection,
 "The Legends of Tono,"</i>

00:04:20.926 --> 00:04:24.063 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> the kappa will attack
 and impregnate human women.</i>

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.865 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> The offspring of these attacks
 are mercilessly killed,</i>

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:29.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> chopped into pieces,
 and buried in wine casks.</i>

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.271 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> There are a lot of regional
 variations of kappa</i>

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:34.607
<i> given its large
 geographic range.</i>

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:37.643 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> They're often depicted
 as an amphibious creature.</i>

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:40.446
<i> Japan is home to over
 30 species of frogs</i>

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:41.780
<i> and 17 types of turtles,</i>

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:45.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> so an amphibious water
 yokai makes sense.</i>

00:04:45.417 --> 00:04:47.053 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> In Eastern Japan,
 kappa commonly</i>

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:48.788 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> had a turtle shell
 on their back,</i>

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.490 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> while in Western Japan,
 variations were more likely</i>

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:53.759 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> to be covered in hair
 and walk upright.</i>

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:56.229
I think these kappa might
be more heavily inspired

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:58.631 align:start size:84% position:16%
by the Japanese
macaque or snow monkey,

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:00.432 align:start size:78% position:22%
who just so happens
to like to take

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.201
hot spring baths in the winter.

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:03.502
<i> The association with kappa</i>

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:05.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> to drowning humans
 seems straightforward.</i>

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:07.606 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> They might've been
 used to warn children</i>

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:09.408 line:0%
<i> to be careful around water.</i>

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:10.876 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> (Yoshiko)
 Think of the kappa legend</i>

00:05:10.976 --> 00:05:14.814 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
<i> as ancient Japan's version
 of a no swimming sign.</i>

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:18.450 align:start position:0% line:0%
<i>Back in the day, a written
warning wouldn't cut it, right?</i>

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:21.020 line:0%
<i>Since most folks couldn't read.</i>

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.490 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> But a chilling tale of a
 life snatching water creature</i>

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.226 line:0%
<i> would stick in your head.</i>

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:29.528
And they're a reminder
to protect livestock.

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:32.164
Those are valuable resources
you wouldn't want to lose.

00:05:32.264 --> 00:05:35.133 align:start size:88% position:13%
Makes sense, but there's
a bit more to it.

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:36.635
<i> In Northern and Eastern Japan,</i>

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:39.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> the kappa more commonly
 pulled horses into the water.</i>

00:05:39.638 --> 00:05:41.407
<i> In these regions,
 horse populations</i>

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.409
<i> were much higher than cows.</i>

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.145
<i> Even going as far back
 as the 13th century,</i>

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:49.548 align:start position:0%
<i>there's evidence of horse
veterinarian clinics in temples,</i>

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:52.018 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> a clear sign
 of the equine's importance.</i>

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:53.686
<i> But over in Western Japan,</i>

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:57.156 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the stories center around
 kappa attacking cows.</i>

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:58.324
<i> This region's leather trade</i>

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:00.592 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> was largely dominated
 by cow skins,</i>

00:06:00.692 --> 00:06:03.262
<i> and boasted the epicenter of
 the Japanese leather trade.</i>

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:06.665 align:start size:97% position:3%
Whether cows or horses, the
kappa's threat is significant.

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:08.801 align:start size:81% position:19%
In the 17th century,
an unparalleled

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:10.736
set of laws existed
to protect these

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:13.239
and other animals
from maltreatment.

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.541 align:start size:88% position:13%
It was illegal to abandon
a sick cow or horse,

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:17.509
and certain rules existed around

00:06:17.609 --> 00:06:19.745
how to dispose of their corpses.

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:22.582
In the late 18th century,
reports emerged of peasants

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:25.284 align:start size:91% position:9%
in rural communities being
charged additional fees

00:06:25.384 --> 00:06:28.054 align:start size:88% position:13%
for corpse removal if
the animal dies in fires

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:29.655
or from drowning.

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:31.357
A kappa pulling a horse or cow

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.692 align:start size:81% position:19%
to a watery grave
would be significant.

00:06:33.792 --> 00:06:37.296
It meant a loss of a valuable
asset and a potential fine.

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:38.797
<i> In the early 18th century,</i>

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:41.167
<i> written folklore of
 the kappa developed,</i>

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:44.670 align:start position:0%
<i>and it gave us the first
recorded images of the monster.</i>

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.506
<i> Around 1713, Terajima Ryoan</i>

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.443 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> compiled a truly
 extensive encyclopedia.</i>

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:52.879
<i> At a whopping 105 volumes,</i>

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:55.214 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> the entries are accompanied
 by illustrations,</i>

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:58.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> including his take on
 a creature labeled kawataro,</i>

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:00.352
<i>which is widely considered to be</i>

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:02.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> the first illustration
 of a kappa.</i>

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.592
<i> This important entry into
 yokai lore is very detailed.</i>

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:09.962
<i>Described by Ryoan as about the
size of a 10-year-old child,</i>

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:12.565 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> it speaks in a human voice,
 walks upright,</i>

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:15.935
<i> and has a partially concave
 head that can hold water.</i>

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:17.703
<i> While they favor
 a watery habitat,</i>

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:19.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> they are not above making
 their way onto land</i>

00:07:20.005 --> 00:07:22.942
<i> to steal crops or enjoy
 a little sumo wrestling,</i>

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:24.243
<i> a sport they excel in,</i>

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.612 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> as long as the top of their
 head is holding water.</i>

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:29.748 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> Ryoan also mentions
 the butt stuff,</i>

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:31.616
<i> as in sucking blood from cows</i>

00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:33.919
<i>and humans through their butts.</i>

00:07:34.019 --> 00:07:36.655 align:start size:81% position:19%
That might sound
ridiculous to us now,

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:38.958
but keep in mind that
yokai were presented in

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:42.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
academic texts alongside
real world animals.

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:46.198 align:start size:91% position:9%
This blurred the lines
between folklore and fact.

00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:48.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> That tradition would
 change slightly in 1776</i>

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.002 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> with the publication
 of the first</i>

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:54.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> yokai-exclusive bestiary
 in Japanese history,</i>

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.543
<i> and you better believe
 the kappa is in there.</i>

00:07:57.643 --> 00:08:00.546 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Author and artist,
 Toriyama Sekien, also added</i>

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:04.517
<i> another detail to existing
 kappa lore: webbed feet.</i>

00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:06.085 align:start size:81% position:19%
With the publication
of this text,

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:08.387
the kappa became more
rooted in folklore

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:10.089
rather than real history,

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:12.892 align:start size:88% position:13%
and less associated with
danger and deviance,

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.461
serving more as symbols
of Japan's rural history.

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:18.731 align:start size:91% position:9%
And that symbolism
carried into modern times.

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:21.366 align:start size:81% position:19%
For example, the 1923
novella, "Kappa,"

00:08:21.466 --> 00:08:23.969
written by Ryunosuke Akutagawa,

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:26.338 align:start size:81% position:19%
uses the fantastical
land of the kappa

00:08:26.438 --> 00:08:27.806
as an intentional caricature

00:08:27.906 --> 00:08:31.176
of post-World War I
Japanese culture.

00:08:31.276 --> 00:08:32.578
<i> Other 20th century creatives</i>

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:34.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> also depict the
 creatures walking upright</i>

00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:37.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> and acting in more
 human-like activities,</i>

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:40.553
<i> a trend that dominated the
 kappa of popular culture</i>

00:08:40.653 --> 00:08:42.988 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> after Shimizu Kon's
 "Kappa Tengoku"</i>

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.758 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> introduced the kappa
 into manga in the 1950s.</i>

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:48.694 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The series centers
 around a family of kappa</i>

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:50.896 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> engaging in everyday
 human behaviors.</i>

00:08:50.996 --> 00:08:53.965 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> They eat,
 shop, and go to work.</i>

00:08:54.065 --> 00:08:55.600 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> It was in this
 decade that Shimizu,</i>

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:57.269
<i> along with artist, Kojima Ko,</i>

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.438
<i> standardized the female kappa.</i>

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:01.140
Making kappa more
human pushes them

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
further away from being
a monster to fear.

00:09:03.775 --> 00:09:05.477
Lighthearted versions continued

00:09:05.577 --> 00:09:07.646
to appear throughout the 1970s,

00:09:07.746 --> 00:09:09.682 align:start size:91% position:9%
where they recalled
a nostalgia for rural life

00:09:09.782 --> 00:09:11.950 align:start size:78% position:22%
and traditional
Japanese folklore.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.720
Rural regions of Japan
publicized kappa legends

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.055 align:start size:88% position:13%
to encourage appreciation
of traditional

00:09:17.155 --> 00:09:20.325
Japanese communities and
boost their economies.

00:09:20.425 --> 00:09:23.662
One of the most popular kappa
tourist destinations is Tono,

00:09:23.762 --> 00:09:25.998
which just so happens
to be an important site

00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:29.068 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
for the historic Japanese
horse trade back in the day.

00:09:29.168 --> 00:09:32.270 line:0%
<i> Kappa continued to
 appear everywhere.</i>

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:36.742 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> Anime, manga, books, and live
 action and animated films.</i>

00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:38.777 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> They appear in
 advertisements and logos</i>

00:09:38.877 --> 00:09:41.580 line:0%
<i> for everything from
 sake to credit cards.</i>

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.115 line:0%
<i> Their more nefarious origins</i>

00:09:43.215 --> 00:09:46.619 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> have turned into
 a nostalgic nod to the past.</i>

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:49.455 line:0%
<i> And they sometimes move
 beyond Japan's borders.</i>

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:52.524 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> In 1993, "Teenage Mutant
 Ninja Turtles III"</i>

00:09:52.624 --> 00:09:54.392 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> takes the anthropomorphic
 turtle brothers</i>

00:09:54.493 --> 00:09:56.161 line:0%
<i> through a time traveling plot,</i>

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:59.532 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> where they are constantly
 mistaken as kappa.</i>

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:00.833 line:0%
<i> Kappa even popped up
 in everyone's favorite</i>

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:04.870 line:0%
<i> cozy escapism game,
 "Animal Crossing."</i>

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:07.239
<i> We also see evidence of
 the kappa's popularity</i>

00:10:07.339 --> 00:10:10.809 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> in a form of modern day
 encyclopedia, the database.</i>

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.411
<i> If there's a fandom out there,</i>

00:10:12.511 --> 00:10:14.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> you can be sure there's
 a fan base website describing</i>

00:10:15.047 --> 00:10:18.083 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> and categorizing the
 minutia of its universe.</i>

00:10:18.183 --> 00:10:21.854 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> The Pokemon wiki,
 monster wiki, or the D&amp;D wiki,</i>

00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:24.790
<i> the kappa appears
 in all of those.</i>

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.692
These collections of fan
knowledge aren't just about

00:10:26.792 --> 00:10:30.496 align:start size:88% position:13%
modernizing the bestiary
or encyclopedia,

00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:33.298
they're part of a global
desire for connection.

00:10:33.399 --> 00:10:34.833
The appreciation
for the folklore

00:10:34.934 --> 00:10:37.636
and its evolution can be
shared around the world.

00:10:37.736 --> 00:10:39.438
And kappa in particular holds

00:10:39.538 --> 00:10:41.573 line:0%
special meaning in
Japanese history.

00:10:41.673 --> 00:10:43.341 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
<i> (Yoshiko)
 As I stated in my book,</i>

00:10:43.441 --> 00:10:48.147 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> "Japanese Mythology in Film,"
 we have this innate desire</i>

00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:51.283 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
<i> to preserve our
 cultural traditions,</i>

00:10:51.384 --> 00:10:54.286 line:0%
<i>our ancestors' beliefs, customs,</i>

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:56.455 line:0%
<i> and even spiritual practices.</i>

00:10:56.555 --> 00:11:01.126 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
<i> These are like the tapestry
 of ancestral wisdom,</i>

00:11:01.226 --> 00:11:04.329 line:0%
<i> woven with newly
 enriched thread,</i>

00:11:04.429 --> 00:11:09.001 line:0%
<i> as they are passed down from
 generation to generation.</i>

00:11:09.101 --> 00:11:10.402
I don't know, something tells me

00:11:10.502 --> 00:11:12.537
Terajima Ryoan would be proud.

00:11:12.637 --> 00:11:13.672 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
I just said boasted,
not boosted.

00:11:13.772 --> 00:11:15.140 line:0%
It is boasted!

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.376 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
'Cause it would've
<i> boosted</i> the leather trade,

00:11:17.476 --> 00:11:19.444 line:0%
but it boasted.

00:11:19.544 --> 00:11:21.179 line:0%
David, you sound so positive.

00:11:21.279 --> 00:11:23.215 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
-We gotta go back to the top.
<i> -We got it, we got it!</i>

